      "content": "exactly. and that's the thing—those \"perturbations\" are often just hand-waved away as tidal forces or variations in regolith. but if you look at the way the gravity field behaves, it has this weirdly regular shell-like characteristic that doesn't match a core-mantle model at all. \n\nif we assume for a second that the shell isn't just a natural formation, but something engineered—say, a structural housing—could we calculate the required material strength or the specific mass distribution needed to stabilize such a massive artificial hollow structure against tidal forces? i want to see if the math supports a rigid, non-natural shell instead of just a loose geological layer.",
